/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:20:\caption{Encoder ablation on SWE-Agent conf${\geq}$2 ($N{=}1{,}824$). All models use the same GRU architecture (2-layer, hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1, lr$=$1e-3, batch$=$32, early stopping patience~5). All encoders exceed 0.90 macro-F1, confirming the probe's temporal architecture drives performance rather than a specific encoder. BGE-M3 achieves the highest score ($+$3.6\,pp over Qwen3), but the gap between encoders (0.5\,pp) is small relative to the structural--probe gap (8.96\,pp).}

tool_args.old_string:
Silver labels are produced by majority vote:
\begin{equation}
y_i = \text{MV}\bigl(\lambda_1(w_i),\; \lambda_2(w_i),\; \lambda_3(w_i)\bigr)
\label{eq:mv}
\end{equation}
We define the confidence of each label as the number of agreeing LFs: $\text{conf}_i = |\{j : \lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\}| \in \{1, 2, 3\}$.
With three binary LFs, majority vote always produces a winner; conf${=}1$ arises when one LF votes opposite to the other two plus abstains differently, producing the most ambiguous labels.

tool_args.new_string:
Silver labels are produced by majority vote among non-abstaining LFs:
\begin{equation}
y_i = \text{MV}\bigl(\{\lambda_j(w_i) : \lambda_j(w_i) \neq \bot\}\bigr)
\label{eq:mv}
\end{equation}
We define the confidence of each label as the number of non-abstaining LFs that agree with the winning label: $\text{conf}_i = |\{j : \lambda_j(w_i) \neq \bot \;\text{and}\; \lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\}| \in \{1, 2, 3\}$.
Conf${=}1$ arises when a single non-abstaining LF determines the label---either because only one LF votes or because two LFs disagree with no clear majority---producing the most ambiguous labels.
